BPF memory cgroup accounting on 4.19
===================================

The BPF map/program RLIMIT_MEMLOCK charge is removed, including when MEMCG
is disabled, matching the 5.15 policy. The retained map page estimates only
supply legacy fdinfo; they are not a cgroup usage counter. Program creator
UID references remain for program information.

Map creation storage and BPF program/aux/stat allocations use ACCOUNT flags.
Maps pin their creator memcg for subsequent hash, trie, local storage,
timer, devmap, cpumap and sockhash allocations, including interrupt updates.
Charge scopes restore prior process/interrupt context on allocation failure.
Offline creators fall back to their nearest online ancestor.

4.19 dependencies:

* Prime per-memcg kmalloc caches before map/program creation. The old slab
  allocator otherwise allows initial allocations into global caches while
  asynchronously creating cgroup caches. Failed preparation rejects creation.
* Per-CPU objects share backing pages. The allocator tracks accounted objects
  and charges their CPU-expanded, page-rounded logical sizes to the native
  memory/kmem counters. Metadata failure or allocation failure rolls back;
  free_percpu releases the charge regardless of the freeing task's cgroup.
  This intentionally uses 4.19 page granularity, rather than importing 5.15's
  byte-granular obj_cgroup allocator. Small objects can cost more than 5.15.
* Native 4.19 slab accounting releases slab pages when caches release them;
  usage need not fall immediately when a single object is freed. Removed
  cgroups can retain outstanding charges until the allocations are freed.

Host regression (kernel allocators, CSS and locks are stubbed):

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_memcg_scopes.py
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_memcg_percpu.py

Device regression:

Compile memcg_accounting_user.c with an Android ARM64 compiler and exported
UAPI from this tree. Run as root in a dedicated memory cgroup with sufficient
headroom; keep unrelated workload out of both measurement cgroups. The test
sets its own MEMLOCK limit to zero, creates array/percpu/dynamic hash maps,
and pauses for inspection and migration into a second cgroup before updates.
Use memory.usage_in_bytes and memory.kmem.usage_in_bytes on cgroup v1, or
memory.current on v2. Dynamic growth should belong to the creator, not the
updating task's new cgroup. After exit, wait for deferred map frees and
allocator reclaim before comparing baseline usage. Repeat with low memory
limits to verify failures and rollback, with the original cgroup removed
while maps remain pinned, and with programs that allocate timer/local storage
from IRQ contexts. Host tests do not validate those kernel execution paths.

Accounting needs MEMCG and MEMCG_KMEM enabled and active at runtime. Remove
boot arguments such as cgroup_disable=memory or cgroup.memory=nokmem to test
it. This patch does not change defconfig or the boot command line. With
accounting disabled, allocations still work but are not cgroup charged.
